  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nUL high centered over the Four Corners continues to dominate our \nweather with dry, subsiding flow being advected into the CWA. This\npattern has greatly limited storm coverage with only isolated \nstorms and mainly over the Gila. More or less this pattern will \nhold steady through Tuesday though the 0Z HRRR is showing \nincreased coverage in the lowlands Tuesday evening. I will \ndiscount it for now since the HRRR has had a notable wet bias for \nday 2 lately.\n\nBy Wednesday, we are still expecting a pattern shift as the H500 \nhigh shifts over TX and OK, extending across the Gulf Coast. This \nwill turn our flow from the south, tapping into subtropical \nmoisture. Dewpoints will climb back above 50 with PW values \nclimbing back above an inch (NBM mean generally ranges 1.1-1.3\"). \nThe increased moisture will bring shower and thunderstorm chances \nback into the picture, lasting through at least the end of the \nweek. Additionally, both the GFS and ECMWF show a few systems \nriding up into area, which would enhance thunderstorm chances. \nMost notably, the GFS shows a substantial wave moving up from \nMexico Wednesday night into Thursday and more or less stalling \nover NM. The ECMWF has a much weaker version of this feature and \nkeeps it over AZ. Both models show another fairly substantial wave\nfor next weekend but here too, both models differ in its \nevolution and track.",
